# General Development Guidelines

## UI formatting and graphic consistency

- Use only the shared color tokens defined in `app/src/main/java/com/hibiki/ui/theme/Color.kt` (`Cyberpunk.*`); do not define or introduce page-local colors in screens or components. For alpha variants use `Cyberpunk.withAlpha`.
- Maximize reuse of existing UI building blocks before creating new ones.
- Prefer generalized, reusable components over one-off page-specific implementations whenever the pattern can be shared.
- Avoid making each page visually independent: shared components and shared styling should be the default to preserve constant graphic consistency across the app.

## Commit and push

- When the user asks to push: **first** bump the app version per `.cursor/rules/version-bump-on-commit.mdc` (same commit as the work—never a follow-up bump after push), commit **all** local work (every modified and untracked file that belongs in the repo — not only the latest chat topic), then push **everything** that is ahead of remote. Do the bump → verify → stage → commit steps **sequentially** (not in parallel tool calls). Do not leave unrelated WIP uncommitted or unpushed. Exclude only secrets and files that must not be tracked (e.g. `.env`, `local.properties`, credentials).

## Other

- Prefer small, localized changes that fit the existing architecture before introducing new abstractions.
- Avoid hardcoding secrets, API keys, or machine-specific paths in tracked files.
- Prefer clear naming and simple control flow over clever or highly compressed code.
- Add focused tests only when they materially reduce regression risk; avoid noisy tests with little value.
- Update project documentation when a structural or workflow convention changes.
